Yes—the light coming through the leaf is generally dimmer and spectrally changed, but it isn’t quite that each surviving ray has been “weakened.”

The leaf absorbs some incoming photons—especially in parts of the red and blue spectrum—and uses a small fraction of their energy to drive photosynthesis. Much of the light is instead reflected, scattered, or transmitted. The photons that make it through still have essentially the same energy per photon; there are just fewer of them, and the mix of colors has shifted. That’s why backlit leaves often glow green or yellow-green.

So your intuition is right: the leaf has intercepted some of the sunlight and put a little of it to work. What reaches the other side is reduced and filtered light.